# GitHub Copilot entry

Use the workspace policy in [AGENTS.md](../AGENTS.md) for authority, Jira scope, checkout ownership, shared skills, and task routing. Preserve user and concurrent-agent changes and follow the current repository workflow.

Copilot loads matching .github/instructions files using their applyTo metadata. These patterns are Copilot-specific. Cursor uses .cursor/rules MDC metadata; other harnesses read a relevant instruction or skill through their own native router. Do not assume that a Markdown link or applyTo field loads content in another harness.

Use .github/skills as the canonical skill source. Select a skill by its description and task trigger; use role personas only when the work needs that role’s expertise. Do not load a full persona for routine edits.

Run commands in the shell selected for that tool. A Fish code block is an interactive Fish example; it does not constrain zsh/POSIX agent tools, and agent examples must not be pasted blindly into another shell. Follow the target repo's Python, SQL, JavaScript/TypeScript, YAML, and infrastructure conventions independently.

Resolve WORKING_ROLE only through [working-context.instructions.md](instructions/working-context.instructions.md). Memory or session history is optional and capability-dependent; never request a tool that the active runtime does not provide.

Architectural changes: read the ADR status router at ../_docs/adr/README.md. Accepted ADRs govern their scope; engineering-effect decisions retain their named approver and are changed only through an explicitly superseding ADR.

Before changing a legacy backend endpoint or service, read the retired/frozen service registry linked by backend-service.instructions.md. Production traffic can outlive an in-repository caller migration.

Prefer the smallest solution that reuses existing code, the standard library, or a native platform feature. Avoid unrequested abstractions and dependencies.
